From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Junod <donujp@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:11:58 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] fix(dht_utils): bound the work retained while resolving
 magnet metadata

read_metainfo_from_peer_receiver wraps each peer connection in a
128-permit semaphore, but it still creates and keeps one future per
candidate address before the semaphore is taken, and it keeps every
unique address in `seen`. The semaphore limits the I/O in flight, not
the number of queued futures or retained candidates, so a DHT/tracker
stream that produces peers faster than they are tried grows without
bound while a magnet is resolving.

Replace the semaphore with explicit queues:

 - at most 128 metadata connections are running (futures are created
   only when a slot is free);
 - up to 256 further addresses wait in a pending queue; while both are
   full, the address stream and the initial address list are not
   polled, so the producers see backpressure;
 - `seen` stops growing at 4096 addresses. Past that, new addresses are
   still tried (they are just no longer de-duplicated), so reaching the
   limit never ends or stalls the search, and the debug log notes it
   once.

Initial addresses go through the same admission path as streamed ones
instead of being pushed up front.

Tests cover the exact queue and de-duplication boundaries, and that
both an initial and a streamed candidate are actually connected to
(loopback listeners).
